    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Can You Use Regular Concrete Blocks for a Fire Pit?
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Stacking attractive retaining wall blocks from Home Depot or Lowe's in a backyard circle is the most popular DIY fire pit project. But without proper thermal shielding, regular concrete blocks can crack, crumble, and in extreme cases, explode. Here is the science of thermal shock and how to build a fire pit that lasts safely for decades.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Campfire Core Temp</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">800°F to 1,200°F</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Standard Block Limit</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-red-600 mt-0.5">Degrades at 400°F–500°F</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Safe Solution</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">Steel Insert Ring or Firebrick</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why Regular Concrete Blocks Fail Under Fire</h2>
    <p>
      Standard landscaping blocks are made of Portland cement, sand, and aggregate cured with water. They are engineered to hold back retaining wall soil—not direct wood flames:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Trapped Moisture &amp; Steam Expansion:</strong> Concrete contains microscopic moisture inside its pores. Water expands to <strong>1,600 times its volume</strong> when converted into steam. When intense flames heat the block face, that expanding steam cannot vent quickly enough through the dense matrix, blowing chunks of concrete off the face (a phenomenon called <em>thermal spalling</em>).</li>
      <li><strong>Chemical Dehydration:</strong> At temperatures above 400°F, calcium silicate hydrate (the chemical glue that gives concrete its strength) begins to chemically break down. The block turns into brittle, dusty chalk that crumbles within 3 seasons.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Rocks That Literally Explode</h2>
    <p>
      Never, under any circumstances, line the base of a fire pit or place decorative landscaping rocks inside the flame zone that belong to the following categories:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-red-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-red-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-red-900 uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Dangerous High-Explosion Hazard Rocks:</h3>
      <ul class="text-xs text-red-800 space-y-1 list-disc pl-4 mb-0">
        <li><strong>River Rocks &amp; Smooth Creek Cobbles:</strong> Highly porous and waterlogged from riverbeds. They will violently shatter into sharp shrapnel.</li>
        <li><strong>Limestone &amp; Sandstone:</strong> Sedimentary stones with layered water pockets that split violently along bedding planes.</li>
        <li><strong>Slate &amp; Shale:</strong> Flakes off and fires flying shards when heated.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe Fire Pit Media:</strong> Only use porous, vesicular <strong>volcanic lava rock</strong> (which was formed at thousands of degrees in magma) or certified tempered <strong>fire glass</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The Two Safe Ways to Build with Concrete Blocks</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-4 not-prose my-6">
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-base mb-1">Method 1: Heavy-Gauge Steel Fire Ring Insert (Most Popular)</strong>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-2">Build your outer retaining wall block ring to a 38-inch or 46-inch inside diameter. Drop in a prefabricated 10-gauge carbon steel or stainless steel fire ring (36\" or 44\" diameter) with an integrated top flange that rests on the top blocks.</p>
        <div class="text-xs text-emerald-800 font-semibold bg-emerald-50 p-2.5 rounded border border-emerald-200">
          Why it works: The steel ring absorbs the direct flame temperature and creates a 1-inch insulating air cushion between the hot steel and the concrete blocks.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-base mb-1">Method 2: True Refractory Firebrick Lining</strong>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-2">Line the inside face of your concrete blocks with 2.5-inch yellow refractory firebricks (ASTM C27) laid in high-temperature refractory mortar (like Heatstop II or S-330).</p>
        <div class="text-xs text-emerald-800 font-semibold bg-emerald-50 p-2.5 rounded border border-emerald-200">
          Why it works: Firebrick is fired in industrial kilns at 2,000°F and can easily withstand continuous 1,500°F wood embers with zero degradation.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="David Miller, PE"
    role="Structural & Civil Engineering Consultant"
    experience="20+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Leave Combustion Air Vents on the First Course"
    note="A common complaint with DIY block fire pits is that they smoke uncontrollably. When you build a 16-inch high solid circular wall, oxygen cannot reach the base of the fire. The fire starves for air, burns cool, and throws smoke straight into everyone's eyes. When stacking your first course of blocks, leave four 2-inch vertical gaps (one at each cardinal compass point: North, South, East, West). These act as natural induction vents: rising hot air draws fresh cool oxygen through the bottom gaps directly into the hot coal bed, creating a clean, smokeless secondary burn."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Fire Pit Construction"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Using Standard Liquid Nails or Polyurethane Glue on the Fire Zone",
        costImpact: "$450 (Toxic Off-Gassing Fumes & Melting)",
        description: "Standard subfloor or construction adhesive softens and burns off at 150°F, producing toxic chemical fumes and causing blocks to separate.",
        solution: "Use only high-temperature landscape block adhesive rated for 400°F+ (such as SRW Retaining Wall Adhesive) applied only to the cooler outer perimeter of the top capstones."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Building Under Low-Hanging Tree Canopies",
        costImpact: "$5,000+ (Tree Fire & Property Damage)",
        description: "Embers and intense heat plumes rise 20 feet above a wood fire pit. Building beneath pine, oak, or cedar canopies will dry out foliage and trigger tree crown fires.",
        solution: "Maintain at least 25 feet of clear overhead clearance above the fire pit, pruning back all overhanging branches."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Filling the Bottom with Pea Gravel",
        costImpact: "$300 (Popping Gravel & Clogged Drainage)",
        description: "Pea gravel contains rounded river stones that pop violently when hot embers land on them, while ash mixes with the small pebbles to form an impermeable concrete-like slurry.",
        solution: "Use coarse 1-inch to 2-inch porous volcanic lava rock for the bottom 4 inches; lava rock breathes, drains water instantly, and never pops."
      }
    ]}
  />

